---
/**
 * website/src/components/ArchitectureDiagram.astro — the flow (website plan, Task W1.4
 * step 3; redesign part P8, findings RF12, RF20 and RF21; decision D185, superseded in part
 * by the changes spec of 2026-10-18, item 4).
 *
 * **The four steps the changes spec names.** The owner's second brief draws the run as
 * `1 Your agent` -> `2 Fugaro` -> `3 Ephemeral cloud job` -> `4 Pull request`, one short
 * line each, and this component is that picture. It supersedes D185's step names (which
 * drew the flow as `task submitted -> ephemeral Cloud Run job -> agent runs -> pull
 * request`): the spec's item 4 is the later ruling, and its four steps say what the site is
 * for — a reader's agent is where a task starts, and Fugaro is what carries it to the
 * cloud, rather than a `fugaro run` invocation the reader is asked to type. `fugaro run`
 * stays in the documentation as the manual option; `website/test/home.mjs` reads the four
 * names out of the spec's own item 4 rather than out of a copy of them here.
 *
 * **Every caption is a line the repository already writes.** A caption goes through
 * `comparable()` below at build time and the build stops, naming the file, when it is not in
 * the section it cites; `website/test/home.mjs` makes the same check against the built page
 * from outside. Nothing here is written for the page.
 *
 * **Why a caption cites a section rather than the whole file.** All four captions are lines
 * of README.md, so held to the file alone they could be shuffled among the steps and each
 * would still be a quotation — while the page then said Fugaro is where the pull request
 * opens. Each cites the section it belongs to (the agent's line is from `## Getting
 * started`, Fugaro's from `## Why Fugaro`, the job's and the pull request's from `## How it
 * works`), and inside one section the lines must appear in the order the section writes
 * them, which is the rule `website/test/home.mjs` holds this page to: of the two steps that
 * share a section, the job's line comes first in the README and must come first here.
 *
 * **The flow is a list, and its picture is decoration.** The steps are an `<ol>` of
 * headings and captions, which is the whole content, and each step's arrow is an SVG marked
 * `aria-hidden`, because a picture of four boxes and three arrows is not a thing a screen
 * reader should have to describe when the four boxes are right there as text. Nothing about
 * the flow depends on the arrows having been drawn. The four cards are one row from 64 rem
 * up, and a column below it, so a phone reads the run top to bottom; the arrows turn with
 * the layout and the step's own heading stays one row at 390 px.
 *
 * **Why 64 rem, and not the chrome's 48 rem.** Four cards share the row, so a card is a
 * quarter of the column less the arrows between them, and at 48 rem that leaves a card's
 * content box about 100 px — narrower than the word `Ephemeral` in the spec's own third step
 * name, at the size a step name is set. The row is where the reader gets four boxes and their
 * arrows travelling left to right, so it is worth having earlier rather than later; it is not
 * worth having a picture that cannot print its own labels. From 64 rem the box measures
 * about 164 px, the longest word fits and a longer name wraps inside its card.
 * `website/test/base-styles.mjs` does that arithmetic over the built stylesheet, at the
 * breakpoint and at the page's maximum width, so the two numbers move together: a name that
 * grows past what a card holds, or a row that starts earlier, fails there naming the card's
 * width.
 *
 * **The name may wrap, and the number does not take its room.** A step name is a heading and
 * not a label, so it wraps rather than running out of its card — `white-space: nowrap` here
 * was the bug the arithmetic above was written for — and inside the row the number sits
 * *above* the name instead of beside it, because the number is `flex: none` and 40 px out of
 * a 164 px box is the difference between a name that fits and one that breaks mid-word.
 *
 * **The animation.** The steps fade in as the reader scrolls to them, using a scroll-driven
 * animation (`animation-timeline: view()`), which costs no JavaScript at all. It is inside a
 * feature query and a preference query: a browser that cannot run scroll-driven animations,
 * and a reader whose system asks for less motion, both get the flow as it stands — every
 * step visible, nothing moving (W1.4 step 3, changes spec item 4's reduced-motion rule).
 */
import { readFileSync } from 'node:fs'
import path from 'node:path'
import { REPO_ROOT } from '../content.ts'

/**
 * A string with its markdown markers and its whitespace removed: how a caption and the
 * section it claims to come from are compared. Emphasis and code spans are markers rather
 * than text, and where a document breaks a line or pads a column is its layout rather than
 * its words — most of the README's ASCII diagram is padding, so a caption quoted from it is
 * compared with the drawing taken out.
 */
function comparable(document: string): string {
  return document.replace(/[`*]/g, '').replace(/\s+/g, '')
}

/**
 * One `## <heading>` section of README.md, up to the next `## ` — the same reader
 * `website/test/home.mjs` uses, so the build and the check resolve a section the same way.
 * A heading the README does not carry is a defect in this file rather than in the README:
 * the steps cite sections by name, and a renamed heading has to fail here, naming itself.
 */
function readmeSection(heading: string): string {
  const readme = readFileSync(path.join(REPO_ROOT, 'README.md'), 'utf8')
  const start = readme.indexOf(`\n## ${heading}\n`)
  if (start === -1) {
    throw new Error(
      `website/src/components/ArchitectureDiagram.astro: README.md carries no "## ${heading}" section, and a step's caption is quoted from it (changes spec 2026-10-18, item 4). Either the section was renamed or the caption moved.`,
    )
  }
  const rest = readme.slice(start + 1)
  const next = rest.indexOf('\n## ', 1)
  return next === -1 ? rest : rest.slice(0, next)
}

/**
 * The sections a caption may come from, by the name each step cites. Sections of README.md
 * rather than whole files: the four captions are all lines of the README, so held to the file
 * alone they could be shuffled among the steps with every one of them still a quotation.
 */
const SOURCES: { [name: string]: string } = {
  'README.md#Getting started': readmeSection('Getting started'),
  'README.md#Why Fugaro': readmeSection('Why Fugaro'),
  'README.md#How it works': readmeSection('How it works'),
}

/**
 * The spec's four steps, in its order, each with the section its caption is a line of. The
 * names are the spec's own, spelled as it spells them; the captions are the repository's own
 * sentences, shortened to one line where a step is longer than a line wants to be.
 */
const STEPS = [
  {
    name: 'Your agent',
    source: 'README.md#Getting started',
    // README.md's own sentence about who starts a run: the plugin's skill, in the reader's
    // agent, is where a task goes in.
    caption: "the plugin's working skill launches, watches and diagnoses runs",
  },
  {
    name: 'Fugaro',
    source: 'README.md#Why Fugaro',
    // The sentence that says what Fugaro is not: there is no Fugaro service in between, and
    // what it uses is the reader's own project and credentials.
    caption: 'it uses your Google Cloud project and your model credentials',
  },
  {
    name: 'Ephemeral cloud job',
    source: 'README.md#How it works',
    // README.md's ASCII diagram, the far end of the CLI's arrow in its own words.
    caption: 'one execution per task, then gone',
  },
  {
    name: 'Pull request',
    source: 'README.md#How it works',
    // Step 4 of the README's numbered steps, its first words.
    caption: 'Push and open the pull request',
  },
]

// Hold every caption to the section it cites, in that section's order, here, so a
// paraphrase — or a caption that moved to the step above — stops the build naming the file
// rather than reaching a reader as the repository's own words. `cursor` walks forward
// through a section, so two steps citing the same one can only quote it in the order it
// writes them.
{
  const cursors: { [name: string]: number } = {}
  for (const [index, step] of STEPS.entries()) {
    const section = SOURCES[step.source]
    const line = comparable(step.caption)
    const from = cursors[step.source] ?? 0
    const at = section ? comparable(section).indexOf(line, from) : -1
    if (at === -1) {
      throw new Error(
        `website/src/components/ArchitectureDiagram.astro: step ${index + 1}'s caption ${JSON.stringify(step.caption)} is not a line of ${step.source} at or after the step before it, and every caption in the flow is the repository's own words in the order their document writes them (changes spec 2026-10-18, item 4; Global Constraint 6).`,
      )
    }
    cursors[step.source] = at + line.length
  }
}
---

<figure class="flow" aria-label="How a task becomes a pull request" data-source="docs/website/redesign/changes-spec-2026-10-18.md">
  <ol class="flow-steps">
    {
      STEPS.map((step, index) => (
        <li class="flow-step" data-step={step.name.toLowerCase()} data-source={step.source}>
          <div class="step-card">
            <div class="step-head">
              {/* The step's place in the flow, as a number: the list carries the order, so
                  this is decoration and a screen reader reads the `<ol>` instead. */}
              <span class="step-number" aria-hidden="true">{index + 1}</span>
              <h2 class="step-name">{step.name}</h2>
            </div>
            <p class="step-caption">{step.caption}</p>
          </div>
          {/* The arrow between this step and the next: decoration, and the last step has
              none. It is inside the step rather than between two of them because a list of
              steps may not carry anything but steps. It points down the column and right
              along the row, which is the layout's business rather than the markup's. */}
          {index < STEPS.length - 1 && (
            <svg class="step-arrow" viewBox="0 0 24 32" aria-hidden="true" focusable="false">
              <path class="arrow-line" d="M12 1 V 25" />
              <path class="arrow-head" d="M4 19 L12 29 L20 19" />
            </svg>
          )}
        </li>
      ))
    }
  </ol>
</figure>

<style>
  /* Tokens only (W1.2): the flow's cards, its numbers and its arrows are named through the
     palette rather than given colours of their own. The accent is what makes the picture
     the loudest thing in its section — the filled step numbers, the rail along the top of
     each card and the arrows are all `--accent`, on the ground or on a card (`--accent`
     against `--ground` and against `--surface` are pairs `a11y.mjs` computes at 3:1, and
     `--ground` on the filled number is the 4.5:1 pair for text on the accent). */
  .flow {
    margin: 0;
    max-width: var(--page-max);
  }

  .flow-steps {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    margin: 0;
    padding: 0;
    list-style: none;
  }

  .flow-step {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
  }

  .step-card {
    display: grid;
    gap: var(--space-2xs);
    padding: var(--space-md);
    border: var(--border-width) solid var(--border);
    border-top: calc(var(--border-width) * 4) solid var(--accent);
    border-radius: var(--radius-lg);
    background: var(--surface);
  }

  /* The step's heading row: the number and the name on one line, which is what the column
     of steps has room for at every width it is a column at. The number is a fixed box beside
     the name; the row that shares the width four ways turns this into a column instead (see
     the 64rem block below). */
  .step-head {
    display: flex;
    align-items: center;
    gap: var(--space-xs);
    flex-wrap: nowrap;
  }

  .step-number {
    display: inline-flex;
    align-items: center;
    justify-content: center;
    flex: none;
    width: 1.75rem;
    height: 1.75rem;
    border-radius: var(--radius-pill);
    background: var(--accent);
    color: var(--ground);
    font-family: var(--font-mono);
    font-size: var(--font-size-sm);
    font-weight: var(--weight-semibold);
  }

  /* The name wraps, at every width: it is a heading, and a heading that cannot fit its card
     is a heading that runs out of it. The wrap is what makes the card's width the only thing
     that matters — `website/test/base-styles.mjs` sizes the card against the longest word of
     the names the flow prints, because a word is what wrapping cannot break. */
  .step-name {
    margin: 0;
    font-family: var(--font-sans);
    font-size: var(--font-size-lg);
    font-weight: var(--weight-semibold);
    letter-spacing: var(--tracking-tight);
    color: var(--text);
    /* A name longer than its card — a future step the spec words in one long word — breaks
       rather than running out of the card, the same guard the caption below carries. */
    overflow-wrap: anywhere;
  }

  .step-caption {
    margin: 0;
    color: var(--text-muted);
    font-size: var(--font-size-sm);
    line-height: var(--leading-normal);
    /* A caption is a sentence with a code span in it, and a long unbroken token would push
       the card wider than the page on a phone: it breaks rather than does that. */
    overflow-wrap: anywhere;
  }

  .step-arrow {
    display: block;
    width: 1.5rem;
    height: 2rem;
    margin: var(--space-2xs) auto;
  }

  .arrow-line,
  .arrow-head {
    fill: none;
    stroke: var(--accent);
    stroke-width: 2;
    stroke-linecap: round;
    stroke-linejoin: round;
  }

  /* The row: four cards side by side, which is the run travelling left to right. The steps
     share the width and the arrows sit between the cards, turned to point along the row — the
     drawing's own box turns with them, so the arrow lands inside the gap rather than across a
     card. **The turn is `-90deg`, and the sign is the direction of the run:** the path below is
     drawn pointing down, and a positive angle in CSS turns clockwise, so `90deg` would send the
     arrow leftwards — from each step back at the one before it, against the order item 4 draws.
     `website/test/base-styles.mjs` reads the drawn direction out of the markup and the angle
     out of this stylesheet and refuses an arrow that points the way the run does not travel.

     64 rem rather than the chrome's 48 rem: a card here is a quarter of the column less the
     arrows, and below about 51 rem that box is narrower than the longest word of the spec's
     own step names. `website/test/base-styles.mjs` computes both numbers and fails if the row
     starts before a card can hold a name.

     The head turns into a column as well: the number is `flex: none`, and beside the name it
     takes 40 px of the box the name has to fit in. Above the name it costs a row and nothing
     horizontal, which is what keeps the four names reading as names rather than as fragments. */
  @media (min-width: 64rem) {
    .flow-steps {
      flex-direction: row;
      align-items: stretch;
      gap: var(--space-2xs);
    }

    .step-head {
      flex-direction: column;
      align-items: flex-start;
    }

    .flow-step {
      flex: 1 1 0;
      flex-direction: row;
      align-items: center;
      min-width: 0;
    }

    .step-card {
      flex: 1 1 auto;
      min-width: 0;
      align-content: start;
    }

    .step-arrow {
      flex: none;
      width: 2rem;
      height: 1.5rem;
      margin: 0;
      transform: rotate(-90deg);
    }
  }

  /* The reveal, as the reader scrolls to each step: a scroll-driven animation, which is a
     timeline the browser drives and not a script the page ships. Guarded twice — once for a
     browser that cannot run it, once for a reader who asked for less motion — so that in
     both of those cases the flow is simply there, in full, with nothing moving. */
  @supports (animation-timeline: view()) {
    @media (prefers-reduced-motion: no-preference) {
      @keyframes step-in {
        from {
          opacity: 0;
          transform: translateY(var(--space-xs));
        }
        to {
          opacity: 1;
          transform: none;
        }
      }

      .flow-step {
        animation: step-in linear both;
        animation-timeline: view();
        animation-range: entry 10% cover 40%;
      }
    }
  }
</style>
